  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of the 4-year-old boy, including periorbital and lower extremity edema, significant proteinuria (4+), and low serum albumin (2.6 g/dL), is highly suggestive of nephrotic syndrome. The absence of hematuria and normal serum complement levels further support this diagnosis, indicating that it is not post-infectious glomerulonephritis. In children, the first-line treatment for nephrotic syndrome is prednisone therapy, which is effective in reducing proteinuria and managing edema. Other options such as enalapril and furosemide may be considered later for hypertension or fluid overload, but they are not the primary treatment for nephrotic syndrome. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to initiate prednisone therapy.",
